"domain": "VFD / Variable Frequency Drive Faults & Operation", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "scenario", "correct_answer": "A", "model_answer": "A", "parse_rule": "leading_letter", "is_correct": true, "http": 200, "turn_status": "answered", "basis": "general_reasoning", "trace_id": "anon-61dcf51fb102ff87", "response_time_ms": 4763, "answer_text": "A\n\n- First, de‑rate the VFD according to the manufacturer’s ambient‑temperature derating chart and confirm the reduced current rating is not exceeded.  \n- Verify the drive’s enclosure ventilation: clean filters, ensure adequate airflow, and consider adding external cooling (fan or heat sink).  \n- Check that the ambient temperature sensor is functioning and that the panel’s temperature is actually 45 °C (measure with a calibrated thermometer).  \n- If the derated current is still too low for the load, plan to relocate the drive to a cooler area or improve panel cooling before restarting.  \n\n(Do you have the drive’s specific derating table handy? It will tell you the exact percentage reduction to apply.)"}
